Reactive Lymphadenopathy
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Tender/mobile/soft localized lymph nodes | Temporally linked to recent regional infection or inflammation | Spontaneous resolution within 4-6 weeks

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-022
Reason for Visit: Acute distress over sudden, painful neck swelling requiring urgent evaluation to exclude acute suppurative lymphadenitis or rapidly expanding malignancy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset, painful cervical swelling and systemic fatigue. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out deep neck space infection or aggressive lymphoma, preventing airway compromise or septic spread prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I found this massive, painful lump in my neck this afternoon and I feel incredibly tired and foggy.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with localized and regional enlargement of cervical lymph nodes. The patient reports a recent history of a localized throat infection that resolved three days ago. Today, there is mild localized aching and severe distress regarding the swelling. The patient describes an absence of generalized B-symptoms like profound night sweats or unexplained weight loss.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe health anxiety regarding potential malignancy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/76 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 14 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious but is in no acute distress, frequently palpating their own neck. A review of systemic symptoms incidentally notes unrefreshing adequate sleep, prominent morning sleep inertia, and daytime cognitive slowing, which the patient attributes to current stress and past viral illnesses.
Physical Exam: Head and neck examination reveals tender, mobile, and soft nodes on palpation in the anterior cervical chain. There is marked asymmetry in lymph node distribution, favoring the right side. Mild erythema and warmth are noted over the overlying skin in this acute phase. There is an absolute absence of fixed or matted node characteristics. Oropharynx is clear without tonsillar exudate. No airway deviation.
Environmental Observations: Safe and clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Reactive Lymphadenopathy - Status: Acute inflammatory response secondary to resolved URI.
Differentials:

Acute Suppurative Lymphadenitis - Ruled out due to the absence of extreme fluctuance, intense localized heat, and lack of high systemic fever.

Malignant Lymphoma - Ruled out clinically for acute risk due to the soft, mobile, and highly tender nature of the node, typical of rapid inflammatory expansion rather than silent neoplastic growth.

Deep Neck Space Infection - Ruled out due to normal airway, absence of stridor, normal swallowing, and no trismus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden appearance of a painful, enlarged neck mass caused immense distress and mandated an urgent after-hours evaluation to exclude life-threatening airway or septic complications. Telephone triage correctly identified the risk for acute deep space neck infection or a rapidly evolving suppurative process. A thorough clinical examination confirmed a classic inflammatory lymph node architecture—soft, mobile, and tender—explicitly lacking the hard, matted signs of malignancy or the fluctuance of an abscess. This definitive physical confirmation immediately reassured the patient, safely avoiding an inappropriate and traumatizing emergency department visit for an entirely benign, self-limiting reactive process.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance and conservative management advised. The expected investigation results include an ultrasound demonstrating preserved lymph node architecture with an echogenic hilum and normal vascularity, which would assist in confirming the diagnosis if the node fails to regress.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce mechanical irritation and pain, strictly avoid touching, massaging, or palpating the swollen node; minimizing physical trauma to the lymphatic capsule will actively decrease inflammation and prevent further swelling.
Home-Based Services: None indicated.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in two weeks to ensure complete resolution of node size following clearance of the inciting trigger.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a sudden high fever, inability to swallow saliva, voice changes, or significant difficulty breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; persistent lymphadenopathy beyond four weeks requires formal sonographic and potentially histological evaluation.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves the node reaching peak size rapidly, followed by a very slow, gradual regression over several weeks as the immune response tapers.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if the lump suddenly doubles in size, becomes violently red and throbbing, or if you begin to have severe night sweats that drench your clothes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Antibiotics are unnecessary tonight because the original throat infection has already resolved, and this lump is simply the normal, delayed immune clean-up process taking place.
Actionable Steps: Take over-the-counter anti-inflammatories with food, and apply a warm compress for 15 minutes twice a day to soothe the stretched tissue over the lymph node.

